WEBVTT

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.650
Pokémon World Championships News!

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.490
A special for the impending exhilarating finals!

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.120
The Masters Tournament
finally reaches its climax!

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.600
Today, we present the most in-depth special...

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.370
...you could hope to see about the impending finals!

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.500
Well then, let’s get right to it!

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:04.900
Today, we’re bringing you a special
on the impending finals.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.900
And let us welcome our guest commentator...

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.230
Mr. Great Blaze, the Battle Reviewer.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.820
Thank you for being here today.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.860
I’m gonna Super light this show on fire!

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.450
Take it away!

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.910
This tournament
has been called “a festival of dreams.”

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:26.380
Let’s recap the all-star cast of competitors
who have thrilled all of us Pokémon fans!

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.130
Ranked 8th, Competitor Satoshi!

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.210
100,000 Volts!

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.090
Ranked 7th, Competitor Iris!

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.720
Ononox!
Use Dragon Pulse!

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.890
Ranked 6th, Competitor Alain!

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.890
Dragon Claw!

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.440
Ranked 5th, Competitor Carne!

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.440
Checkmate!

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.230
Moonforce!

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.400
Ranked 4th, Competitor Wataru!

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.400
Destroyer Beam!

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.200
Ranked 3rd, Competitor Daigo!

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.200
Sparkle of stone...

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.740
Become our bond!

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:03.250
Ranked 2nd, Competitor Shirona!

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.250
Mega Evolve!

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.460
Ranked 1st, Competitor Dande!

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.250
Me and my Pokémon
will take down all comers!

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.840
They are the members of the Masters Eight,

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.470
who reign over more than 10,000 Trainers.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.640
Don’t they make an amazing group, Mr. Great Blaze?

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.520
Oh... It’s a miracle! A miracle!

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.050
Witnessing a tournament like this is just...!

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.850
With all the hot battles,
ever since the first round,

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.810
everyone’s probably
singed by now, hmm?

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:32.280
Because of the <i>hot</i> battles!

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.440
Mm-hmm, you’re on fire today.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.200
Now then, next up...

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:43.330
An in-depth look at the key moments from
the fiercely fought semifinals, with Mr. Great Blaze!

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.370
Called...

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.870
“Great Blaze’s
Now That’s SUPER!”

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.670
Well then, over to you.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.130
All right!

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:55.800
First, let’s take a look at the battle between li’l Dande and li’l Carne!

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.840
Until the endgame, Competitor Dande
set the pace of the match.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.850
Competitor Carne struggled
to find a way out.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.270
Mmm... Her strategies
ended up backfiring.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.480
When Competitor Carne
was down to her last Pokémon,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.230
she sent in her ace, Sirknight!

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:18.490
Competitor Dande also sent out Lizardon,
making for a showdown between aces!

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.070
100,000 Volts!

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.330
Now that’s powerful!

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:32.960
Whoa! Kyodaimax Lizardon
was greatly shaken!

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.710
Kyodai Hellfire!

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.640
Competitor Dande picks Kyodai Hellfire
to bring the match to a quick end.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.180
Competitor Carne was trapped!
But then...

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.200
Super!

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.060
Make sure you don’t miss
what happened next!

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.360
Psychokinesis!

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:10.370
What?!

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.790
It stole that Kyodai Hellfire with Psychokinesis!

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.380
That’d usually be impossible.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:22.010
That’s the Super psycho power
of a Champion’s Sirknight!

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:23.010
Mr. Great Blaze,

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:28.220
that was the first time someone had ever blocked
Kyodai Hellfire in an official match.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.730
You may be right there...
Even I hadn’t seen that before.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.060
Li’l Dande must’ve been panicking back then!

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.610
But it was stopped at the last moment!

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.440
Competitor Dande ended up taking the match.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:46.160
Next, if you would go over the battle between
Competitor Shirona and Competitor Satoshi.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.870
Oh, there were so many highlights,
I could go on for hours!

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:53.960
It was a battle where they both
fully played to their strengths, wasn’t it?

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.710
You know it was.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:00.340
Li’l Shirona picked Mikaruge for her first move.
Her strategy was really sharp!

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.260
Yes.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.930
That combo of Hypnosis and Dream Eater...

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.850
Plus, striking first with Surprise Attack...

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:09.770
Competitor Satoshi looked like
he was having a real tough time.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.440
Mikaruge is a Pokémon
of few weaknesses.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.570
She must’ve planned to use Mikaruge
as a starting point...

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:20.890
...to build upon, no matter
what Pokémon li’l Satoshi picked.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:23.150
So that explains it...

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:30.040
Indeed, she went on to swap Pokémon
again and again, sending Mikaruge in at vital points.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.580
And then...

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.500
Super!

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.840
What happened next left me
shaking and shivering— shakevering!

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.590
Don’t worry, Pikachu.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:43.840
I have a plan.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.390
Spin around using 100,000 Volts!

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.860
It’s too early for a victory dance.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.440
Hypnosis!

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:12.240
Wow! Blocked by Pikachu’s 100,000 Volts,
Mikaruge’s Hypnosis can’t reach him!

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.500
That can’t...

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.660
Finish it!

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.000
Pikachu conquers the Hypnosis!

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:27.760
I hear that offensive-yet-defensive
100,000 Volts even astonished Competitor Dande.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.470
I’d never seen anything like it!

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:34.520
So you’re rating his incredible tactic as “Super!”, then?

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.640
Oh, no, no.
That wasn’t it.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.730
The shakevering was from this part!

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.320
Now! Iron Tail!

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.610
Mikaruge, go ahead!

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.370
All right!

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.470
Wh-What is this?

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:13.890
What?!

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:23.770
Pikachu!

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:27.320
It’s Drag Along!

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:40.790
Mikaruge and Pikachu are both
unable to battle!

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.500
Li’l Shirona really showed her scary side.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.170
I wanna watch that again to be sure,

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.550
but li’l Shirona
didn’t even command that move!

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.970
Now! Iron Tail!

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.260
Mikaruge, go ahead!

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:59.100
Oh! You’re right!

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.560
Does that mean she commanded the move
just with that “Go ahead”?

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:06.730
I think she must’ve predicted something like this
and trained for it with Mikaruge.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.490
Her desire to win was truly Super!

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.740
A Champion through and through!

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:17.870
There were more Super moments
in their battle, weren’t there?

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:21.210
I’ve still got plenty of highlights left!

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:26.750
Competitor Satoshi lost his ace, Pikachu,
and had his rhythm completely thrown off,

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:31.800
but he followed it up with Uonoragon
and Negigaknight, who played their parts well,

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:34.680
allowing him to hang on somehow.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.350
Negigaknight’s chivalrous mind...

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.180
Uonoragon’s guts...

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:42.980
Their inner strength
helped him escape his dilemma.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:47.940
Competitor Satoshi builds up
to his final Pokémon, Lucario.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.490
Competitor Shirona sends Togekiss
onto the battlefield.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.320
And then...

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.240
Super!

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:59.200
Li’l Shirona’s usually such an angel,
but she looked downright demonic...

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:02.160
Return, Togekiss!

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:04.710
Huh?!

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.460
What? Yet another recall?

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.220
A Daimax band!

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.800
Tower over all, white wings!

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.980
Daimax Togekiss!

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.490
If I got started on how amazing that was,
I could talk the rest of the show away.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:47.210
But you have to admire the keen judgment
she showed in picking Daimax over Mega Evolution.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:53.170
Emotionally, she must’ve wanted to save Mega Evolution for her ace, Gaburias.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.640
I think she wanted to press the win
while she had the advantage.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:00.890
After all, if li’l Satoshi gets going,
there’s no stopping him.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:05.480
What do you suppose Competitor Shirona’s aim would have been?

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:10.320
Mmm... Well, pushing through with the power of Daimax must’ve been part of it...

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.570
But take a close look.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.070
Bullet Punch!

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:15.450
Dai Jet!

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:19.120
Charge up your Aura!

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:20.490
Dai Jet!

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:28.710
She kept commanding Dai Jets.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:33.050
Yeah! She was raising its speed
with the effect of Dai Jet!

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.680
Speed up enough,
and your opponent can’t land their attacks.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.640
Li’l Shirona, always thinking
several moves ahead...

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.680
I bet you can see the future, can’t you?!

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:45.140
Her Natio-class predictive powers are Super!

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.980
And then,
this next part was just incredible...

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.860
We interrupt this program to bring you breaking news.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.190
Announcer Joshie, please go ahead.

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:56.490
This is a news flash.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:12:03.200
According to an insider, Competitor Shirona had decided to retire as a trainer after this match.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:03.950
What?!

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.870
While the exact reason is unclear,

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:05.370
Hold on a sec!

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.460
Li’l Shirona, you’ve got so much potential!

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:11.040
it is thought to have been to focus on research in her other profession of archaeologist.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:08.710
Mr. Great Blaze?

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:09.830
Please calm down,

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:11.460
I can’t hear the news.

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:14.090
However, after her match with Competitor Satoshi,

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:17.510
she withdrew her intention to retire as a trainer.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.130
She is not retiring.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:22.010
Competitor Shirona will not be retiring.

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:23.600
That concludes the news flash.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.520
Oh, what a relief!

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:30.310
She <i>did</i> seem to be having lots of fun
in her battle with li’l Satoshi!

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:33.860
Maybe that battle reminded her
of the joys of childhood!

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:35.900
Thank you, li’l Satoshi!

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:36.530
Indeed.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:38.620
While it may have interrupted this program,

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:43.240
that was a news flash reporting that
Competitor Shirona will not be retiring.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.870
After the break,
what you’ve been waiting for!

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:50.080
An in-depth look at the two finalists!

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.380
We’ve also obtained
exclusive confidential info to reveal,

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.550
so don’t miss it!

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:01.890
Now, it’s time for our feature on the impending finals!

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:04.640
Today, through our exclusive sources,

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:09.100
we have obtained the latest poster for the tournament ahead of anyone else.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.480
Let’s take a look at it.

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:24.410
“The Climax Concludes”!

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.080
What do you think, Mr. Great Blaze?

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.540
H-Hold on a sec. These are the full teams
heading into the finals?!

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.880
That’s right!
Let’s take a closer look.

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:37.630
If you would start with
Competitor Satoshi’s Pokémon.

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:38.300
Right!

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.300
First...

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:43.550
The yellow lightning of Masara, Pikachu!

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.140
The way it moves is so enchanting.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:49.480
Does it have wings or something?!

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:54.560
It shows impressive versatility in
pulling off Satoshi’s novel insights, too.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.020
Indeed!

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:59.610
That small body has
first class speed and power!

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:01.400
Not to mention its mind.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:05.570
It’s like it has “I’m not gonna lose to
those big guys!” written on its face!

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:08.490
It’s definitely well trained.

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:10.580
Next...

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.830
Aura’s new star, Lucario!

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:18.750
When it Mega Evolves,
its concentration goes way up!

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.860
It can already read
its opponents’ moves with Aura,

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.720
but add that into the mix,
and it’s marvelous!

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:25.510
Oh!

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:29.470
It did manage to precisely catch
Togekiss, despite its raised speed.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:30.560
Yeah!

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.600
Its final move was timed perfectly, too!

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:36.980
Li’l Satoshi too, giving the command
like, “It’s now or never!”...

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:39.360
Maybe he can control Aura, too?

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:40.860
Just kidding!

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.280
Next...

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:46.120
The unyielding leek swordsman, Negigaknight!

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:50.790
Great Blaze thinks
he’s the semifinals’ MVP!

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:55.870
Lucario only had its chance to shine
because of Negigaknight, after all.

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:57.920
Destroying the Stealth Rock...

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:59.500
Defeating Milokaross...

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:05.300
And decimating the opposing ace
Gaburias’s stamina with Star Assault!

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:10.810
I bet Negigaknight’s gallant battle
inspired Lucario, too.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:13.560
Next...

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:17.400
The ruler of ancient Galar, Uonoragon!

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:20.400
I’m awarding that one
the hard-worker prize!

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.780
It worked so hard,
not giving in to the poison!

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:26.490
I get the feeling its power
has been rising rapidly lately...

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:31.450
Mmm... Perhaps its ancient senses
are returning to it.

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:35.830
It’s a species without much data,
so I can’t wait to see it battle in the finals!

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:40.130
Oh, it’s a Pokémon even you don’t know
all that much about, Mr. Great Blaze?

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:43.420
I think that’s a mystery
our program should look into!

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.550
Next...

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.680
The elusive black demon, Gänger!

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:54.850
The Will-o-Wisp it learned for the Masters
Tournament has given it a good presence.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:59.610
Get burned and oh, your stamina
gets sapped, your strength falters...

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.980
It’s bad news!

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:05.780
Gänger has Kyodaimax to use
as a trump card, too.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:06.820
Yeah!

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:09.740
Mega Evolution...
Z-Moves... Daimax...

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:13.370
What’s li’l Satoshi
gonna choose to use in the finals?

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:15.700
Lastly...

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:19.290
Meteor incarnate, Kairyu!

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:23.840
Kairyu is sure to be key
in aerial battles in the finals.

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:28.180
Competitor Dande’s Pokémon
are strong at aerial battles, too.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:32.510
Oh, it looks like
it has so much fun flying...

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:34.470
According to an insider report,

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:39.730
Competitor Satoshi is the one
who taught Kairyu how to fly.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:42.520
Huh?! Li’l Satoshi can fly?!

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:45.980
Our program should
look into that mystery, too!

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:51.200
Now then, let’s continue with a look
at Competitor Dande’s Pokémon.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:53.240
I’ve been wondering about those!

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.830
He even has some
he hasn’t shown off in battles before.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:57.370
That’s right!

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:03.590
Lizardon, Gorillander, and Drapult
showed their skills in the semifinals,

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:07.880
but today, his remaining three
have also been revealed.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:11.260
Aceburn, Barrikohru, Inteleon...

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:13.510
That’s a real bunch of tricksters!

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.520
Could you enlighten us about them, then?

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:17.310
Sure!

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.980
Then, first up is Aceburn!

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.020
But wouldn’t you say it’s odd?

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:23.560
Hmm? In what way?

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:28.400
It’s a Fire-type, just like Lizardon.
That’s not exactly balanced.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.860
I can’t tell what li’l Dande’s plotting!

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.530
It really is exciting, isn’t it?

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:34.370
Next is...

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:36.030
...Inteleon, huh?

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.250
Now <i>there’s</i> a skillful Pokémon.

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:41.960
It could be anything at all,
depending on its Trainer’s skills.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:46.630
Which means a Champion’s Inteleon
has no bounds.

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:48.960
Last up is Barrikohru!

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.130
The one I’m most excited to see!

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.510
It doesn’t appear to be
very strong, though...

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:55.760
Ain’t that a good thing?

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:57.640
If it’s li’l Dande’s Pokémon,

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:01.730
it <i>has</i> to be two-faced—
No, belly-faced!

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:05.440
Wow... I really can’t wait!

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:10.070
What sort of battle will it be?
I think it’ll be something I can’t even imagine!

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:15.120
Actually, there’s a special meaning behind these two battling.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:20.950
This program has obtained rare footage diving into these competitors’ past.

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:23.210
Let the battle begin!

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:29.340
This footage is from when Competitor Satoshi and Competitor Dande had an unofficial battle.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:33.090
What is this?!
They’ve already battled before?!

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:39.850
Pikachu!

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.440
All right!
Use 100,000 Volts!

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:49.230
Flamethrower!

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:03.080
Don’t give up!

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:33.650
Lizardon?!

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:41.870
The Kyodaimax was a success!

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:48.380
Li’l Satoshi’s Pikachu
can Kyodaimax?!

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:50.340
That’s right.

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:57.590
Apparently Competitor Satoshi got his
Daimax Band from Competitor Dande that day.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:59.430
Kyodai Heavy Thunder!

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:08.980
Now, enjoy the climax!

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:10.690
Kyodai Hellfire!

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:25.870
Pikachu!

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:36.630
Pikachu!

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:39.130
Pikachu is unable to battle!

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:42.930
As such, the winner is
Competitor Dande!

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:47.230
I like young,
unrefined trainers like you.

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:53.730
But if you want to satisfy me,
you’ll have to <i>pop</i> more!

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:55.230
“Pop”?

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:59.030
Put on more of a show!

00:20:59.030 --> 00:20:59.910
Later, then.

00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:05.450
Mr. Dande!
Please, battle me again someday!

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.210
Our next match will be
an official one, with a crowd!

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:10.580
Yeah!

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:16.920
So the finals will fulfill their promise
from that day at last!

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:24.180
Apparently, the two of them also fought side-by-side
against the Legendary Pokémon Mugendaina.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:29.770
The two stars that protected Galar’s peace
will finally clash in an official battle!

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:34.940
That’s another reason that the upcoming finals
have a very special battle in store.

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:38.360
It’s sure to be the match of the century!

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:42.740
Yeah! Everyone,
let’s cheer them on together!

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:47.450
This is a nice sense of tension...

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:52.330
I wonder why...

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:56.040
...I’m reminded of
the first day I ever battled.

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:03.260
Everyone!

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:06.050
You ready to go?!

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.350
We’re gonna win!

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:13.770
Come on, Satoshi!

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:20.360
Pull yourself together!

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:23.070
We’re here watching you!

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:25.950
Big Bro!

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:26.660
Satoshi...

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:27.530
Satoshi.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:29.370
Satoshi. Go for it!

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:30.580
Now, show me more!

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:34.000
This battle...
Everything since me and Pikachu met...

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:37.590
All our adventures
come together in this battle!

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:05.920
Pokémon World Championships
Masters Tournament News!

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:08.220
It’s finally here!

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:10.390
The Masters Tournament
Finals Battle!

00:24:10.390 --> 00:24:14.020
Competitor Dande’s bound to send out
super strong Pokémon, meow!

00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:15.850
Go for broke, brat boy!

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.980
No matter what he sends out,
you better not lose to that “strongest guy”!

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:23.110
And there you have it! The final showdown
the whole world’s been waiting for is about to begin!

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:24.480
Next time, on Pocket Monsters:

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.490
The Finals I: “Torrent”

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:28.410
Let’s all tune in, meow!
